+# Free-Text Veterinarian Search Stays Out of Scope; Filtering the Directory by Specialty Is In
+
+**Status:** Accepted
+
+## Context
+
+NG-9 declined search for a pet, a visit, or a veterinarian, leaving only owners searchable. Its rationale was that owner search already demonstrates paged prefix search once, and repeating that pattern for other entities would add surface without adding understanding.
+
+A request has now asked to narrow the veterinarian directory to the veterinarians holding a named specialty. That request sits on the boundary NG-9 drew. Filtering a list by a value it already displays is not the same capability as free-text search. It demonstrates a query the directory does not yet show, without reintroducing the paged prefix-search pattern owner search already teaches.
+
+The owner has decided the boundary (2026-08-23).
+
+## Options Considered
+
+1. **Leave NG-9 whole.** Treat the specialty filter as barred search. Rejected: filtering by an attribute the page already shows is a distinct capability from free-text search, and it teaches a query the sample does not otherwise demonstrate.
+2. **Drop NG-9 entirely.** Admit veterinarian search of every kind. Rejected: free-text veterinarian search still only repeats the paged prefix search owner search teaches, adding surface without understanding.
+3. **Narrow NG-9** (chosen). Admit filtering the directory by a specialty it already shows; keep free-text veterinarian search out.
+
+## Decision
+
+NG-9 is narrowed. Filtering the veterinarian directory by a whole, case-insensitive specialty name is in scope and realized by REQ-VET-003. Free-text search for a veterinarian — matching arbitrary text against a veterinarian's name or other free-form attributes — stays out, as does search for a pet or a visit.
+
+The reinstated machine-readable vet list carries the same filter. The withdrawn `REQ-VET-002` stays withdrawn and its id is not reused; the surface it described is now supported under REQ-VET-003.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- NG-9 leaves the open-provenance consultation as a decided row; its narrowed rationale names the boundary. Every other undecided row remains part of that consultation.
+- The veterinarian directory gains a specialty filter on both its HTML page and its machine-readable list. No on-screen control is added in this round; a visible control is a possible follow-up request.
+- Free-text search for a pet, a visit, or a veterinarian remains out of scope. Widening the row further is a recorded owner decision with its own non-goal ADR, per the table's convention.
+
+## Implementation
+
+**Non-goal:** NG-9
+
+- [PRD Non-Goals](../prd.md#non-goals) — the narrowed row and preamble.
+- [REQ-VET-003](../prd.md#req-vet-003) — the specialty filter the narrowing admits.
