@@ -120,13 +120,27 @@ A visit is booked against a particular pet and carries the date it is for and a
 
 The clinic publishes the veterinarians it employs with the specialties each holds, listed a page at a time. A veterinarian holding no specialty is shown as having none rather than left blank `[REQ-VET-001]`.
 
+<a id="req-vet-003"></a>
+
+The directory can be narrowed to the veterinarians holding a named specialty. It is published in two forms — the page a reader browses and a machine-readable list — and both accept an optional specialty to filter by, supplied as a query parameter: `/vets.html?specialty=<name>` for the page and `/vets?specialty=<name>` for the machine-readable list. The machine-readable list is a supported surface for this purpose rather than an unrequested artifact `[REQ-VET-003]`. A specialty matches when it equals a veterinarian's held specialty in full, disregarding letter case — the whole name, not merely its start. On the page, the narrowing applies before the list is paged, so the page count reflects only the matching veterinarians and the paging links carry the specialty as the reader moves between pages. A specialty no veterinarian holds returns the ordinary page or machine-readable list with no veterinarians in it, not an error. A specialty given blank or as nothing but spaces is treated as though none were supplied, the same way an empty owner search is; without the parameter both forms behave as they do today. This round adds no on-screen control for choosing a specialty — the filter is reached only through the query parameter `[REQ-VET-003]`.
+
+**Design Rationale:** See [ADR: Free-Text Veterinarian Search Stays Out of Scope; Filtering the Directory by Specialty Is In](adr/2026-08-23-non-goal-veterinarian-search.md).
+
 **Done when:**
 - `[REQ-VET-001]` given the clinic's veterinarians, when the directory is opened, then each name and its specialties are listed a page at a time.
 - `[REQ-VET-001]` given a veterinarian holding no specialty, when the directory is opened, then that veterinarian is shown as having none.
+- `[REQ-VET-003]` given a specialty some veterinarians hold, when `/vets.html?specialty=<name>` is opened, then only the veterinarians holding that specialty are listed and the page count reflects only them.
+- `[REQ-VET-003]` given a filtered page beyond the first, when a paging link is followed, then the link carries the specialty so the filtered list stays navigable.
+- `[REQ-VET-003]` given a specialty differing from a held specialty only by letter case, when the page or the machine-readable list is filtered by it, then it matches.
+- `[REQ-VET-003]` given a value that is a leading part of a held specialty but not the whole name, when either form is filtered by it, then it matches no veterinarian.
+- `[REQ-VET-003]` given a specialty no veterinarian holds, when either form is filtered by it, then the ordinary page or list is returned with no veterinarians in it and no error.
+- `[REQ-VET-003]` given a blank or whitespace-only specialty, when either form is requested with it, then the result is the same as requesting that form without the parameter.
+- `[REQ-VET-003]` given a specialty some veterinarians hold, when `/vets?specialty=<name>` is requested, then the machine-readable list returns only those veterinarians.
+- `[REQ-VET-003]` given no specialty parameter, when either form is requested, then it behaves as it does today.
 
 **Edge cases:**
 1. A veterinarian's specialties are presented in a stable order rather than an arbitrary one.
-2. **Known defect.** A second route returns the same list in a machine-readable form. Nothing consumes it, and it carries no requirement — see the Superseded list.
+2. The machine-readable list at `/vets` is a supported surface under REQ-VET-003. The withdrawn `REQ-VET-002` — the earlier requirement derived from the same route — stays withdrawn and its id is not reused; see the Superseded list.
 
 **Design:** [system-design.md#contracts](system-design.md#contracts)
 
